type LessonSeed = [string,string,string,string,string[],string,number,string,string];
const extendCourse = (id:string, outcomes:string[], capstone:string, answer:string, href:string, seeds:LessonSeed[]) => {
 masteryCourses[id] = course(outcomes,capstone,answer,seeds.map(([title,idea,conditions,problem,solution,question,result,mistake,explanation])=>unit(title,idea,conditions,
 ['Identify the objects, units and hypotheses in the problem.','Apply the relationship in the worked solution, retaining exact values until the last step.','Check a boundary case and explain what the result means.'],problem,solution,[question,result],mistake,explanation,
 'Predict a parameter change and a boundary case. Record the parameters and observed result; explain which assumptions hold and whether the experiment supports an example or a general proof.',href)));
};
extendCourse('calculus',['Justify limits and convergence with explicit hypotheses.','Bound approximation error and apply oriented integral theorems.'],
 'Approximate e^0.1 by its quadratic Taylor polynomial and bound the error.','The approximation is 1.105. Taylor gives error at most e^0.1(0.1)^3/6 < 0.000185. A small observed error alone is not a bound.','/calculus',[
 ['Epsilon–delta control','A limit controls output error through sufficiently small nonzero input error.','The implication must hold for every point in the punctured neighborhood.','Prove lim(x→2)(3x+1)=7.', ['The output error is 3|x−2|.','For arbitrary ε>0 choose δ=ε/3.','Then 0<|x−2|<δ implies output error <ε.'], 'For ε=0.06, what δ does this proof choose?',.02,'A graph establishes the epsilon–delta definition.','A finite graph suggests behavior; the quantified bound proves it.'],
 ['Absolute and conditional convergence','Absolute convergence implies convergence; the converse fails.','The alternating-series test needs magnitudes decreasing to zero, eventually.','Classify Σ(−1)^(n+1)/n.', ['The magnitudes 1/n decrease to zero, so the alternating series converges.','The absolute-value harmonic series diverges.','Convergence is conditional; rearrangement requires care.'],'The next-term error bound after 10 terms is 1 divided by what?',11,'Terms tending to zero guarantee convergence.','The harmonic series is a counterexample.'],
 ['Green theorem and orientation','The integral of planar curl equals positively oriented boundary circulation.','Use a piecewise smooth simple closed boundary and a C¹ field on its interior and a surrounding open set.','Find circulation of F=(−y,x) around the unit circle counterclockwise.', ['Curl is 2.','The disk has area π.','Green theorem gives 2π; clockwise orientation changes the sign.'],'For radius 2, circulation is what multiple of π?',8,'Reversing the boundary leaves circulation unchanged.','An oriented line integral changes sign under reversal.'],
 ['Constrained extrema','At regular constrained extrema, objective and constraint gradients are parallel.','Check regularity, all candidates and global behavior.','Maximize xy subject to x²+y²=2.', ['(x−y)²≥0 gives 2xy≤2.','Equality holds at x=y=1 and x=y=−1.','The global maximum is 1; x=−y gives minimum −1.'],'Maximum xy when x²+y²=18?',9,'Every stationary point is a maximum.','Candidates require classification or a global bound.']]);
extendCourse('number-systems',['Distinguish representation from value.','Explain completeness and quantify rounding error.'],
 'Can a finite decimal display decide rationality?','A finite display is rational but can approximate either a rational or an irrational exact value. A truncated display cannot decide rationality.','/number-systems',[
 ['Repeating expansions and bases','A reduced rational terminates in base b exactly when its denominator divides a power of b.','Reduce first; the criterion depends on the base.','Does 3/20 terminate in bases 10 and 2?', ['20 divides 10², so decimal 0.15 terminates.','No power of 2 is divisible by 5.','Its binary expansion repeats without terminating.'],'Convert binary 101101 to decimal.',45,'Repeating expansions are irrational.','Eventually periodic positional expansions represent rational values.'],
 ['Completeness and rational gaps','Every nonempty real set bounded above has a real supremum; ℚ lacks this property.','Specify the ambient ordered field.','Consider positive rational q with q²<2.', ['The set is nonempty and bounded above.','Its real supremum is √2.','That irrational supremum does not belong to ℚ.'],'If q=1.4, compute q².',1.96,'Density implies completeness.','ℚ is dense but lacks the least-upper-bound property.'],
 ['Floating point and cancellation','Absolute error is a difference; relative error scales by the nonzero exact value. Subtraction can magnify relative error.','Specify precision; relative error at exact zero is undefined.','Approximate 1/3 by 0.33.', ['Absolute error is 1/300.','Relative error is (1/300)/(1/3)=0.01.','The relative error is 1%.'],'Relative error percent when 100 is approximated by 99?',1,'More displayed digits guarantee accuracy.','Digits may reflect unstable computation rather than reliable precision.']]);
extendCourse('linear-algebra',['Interpret solution spaces and orthogonal projections.','Use singular values and spectral hypotheses.'],
 'Why avoid normal equations for nearly dependent columns?','For full column rank, AᵀA squares the 2-norm condition number. QR or SVD avoids forming that product and exposes small singular values.','/linear-algebra',[
 ['Rank, nullity and solutions','Rank plus nullity equals the number of columns. A consistent solution set is a particular solution plus the nullspace.','Check consistency before discussing free variables.','Solve x+y+z=3.', ['Rank is 1 with three columns.','Set y=s,z=t to obtain x=3−s−t.','The solution set is an affine plane with two free parameters.'],'Nullity of a rank-3 matrix with 7 columns?',4,'Underdetermined systems are always consistent.','Free variables describe solutions only after consistency is established.'],
 ['Least squares and orthogonality','The least-squares residual is orthogonal to the column space.','Unique coefficients need independent columns; the fitted vector is always unique.','Fit a constant to 1,2,6.', ['Minimize the sum of squared residuals.','Differentiation gives c=(1+2+6)/3=3.','Residuals −2,−1,3 sum to zero.'],'Least-squares constant for 2,4,9?',5,'Least squares makes every residual zero.','It minimizes squared error; exact interpolation is separate.'],
 ['Singular values and conditioning','Singular values measure stretching; their ratio measures full-rank sensitivity.','State the norm and rank. Singular square matrices have infinite inverse condition number.','Find the 2-norm condition number of diag(10,0.1).', ['Singular values are 10 and 0.1.','The ratio is 100.','Poorly resolved directions amplify perturbations.'],'Condition number of diag(6,2)?',3,'Large determinant guarantees good conditioning.','Determinant is a product; conditioning is a ratio.'],
 ['Spectral theorem and defect','Real symmetric matrices have orthonormal eigenbases; general matrices may lack enough eigenvectors.','Symmetry is an essential hypothesis.','Analyze [[1,1],[0,1]].', ['The characteristic polynomial is (λ−1)².','The eigenvector equation forces y=0.','Its one-dimensional eigenspace prevents diagonalization.'],'Dimension of this eigenspace?',1,'Real eigenvalues guarantee diagonalization.','Geometric multiplicity may be too small.']]);
extendCourse('complex-numbers',['Handle branches and analyticity.','Evaluate contour integrals using local singularities.'],
 'Why is there no continuous logarithm on all nonzero complex numbers?','Tracking argument around zero changes it by 2π, so the logarithm changes by 2πi upon returning to the same point. A branch cut or restricted domain is needed.','/complex-numbers',[
 ['Logarithmic branches','Logarithms have values ln|z|+i(arg z+2πk). A branch chooses an argument interval.','State the interval and excluded cut.','Find all logarithms of −1.', ['Its modulus is 1.','Arguments are π+2πk.','Logarithms are i(π+2πk).'],'Principal argument of −i as a multiple of π?',-.5,'Principal logs always preserve products as sums.','Crossing a branch cut introduces a 2πi discrepancy.'],
 ['Cauchy–Riemann conditions','For C¹ components on an open set, Cauchy–Riemann equations imply complex differentiability.','Isolated equations without regularity are insufficient.','Test conjugation.', ['Write u=x,v=−y.','u_x=1 but v_y=−1.','The equations fail everywhere.'],'Derivative of z² at z=3?',6,'Real differentiability implies complex differentiability.','All complex approach directions must give the same derivative.'],
 ['Contours and residues','Poles contribute residues multiplied by 2πi and winding number.','Contours avoid singularities; orientation matters.','Integrate 1/z around the unit circle counterclockwise.', ['Use z=e^(it).','Then dz/z=i dt.','The integral is 2πi.'],'For 3/z, the integral is what multiple of 2πi?',3,'Every closed contour integral vanishes.','An enclosed pole violates the analyticity hypothesis.']]);
extendCourse('mathematical-modelling',['State units and assumptions before fitting.','Separate validation, identifiability and sensitivity.'],'Two models fit training data equally well. How should you choose?','Compare held-out prediction, physically meaningful parameters, residual patterns and stability under perturbation. Training fit alone does not identify the mechanism.','/mathematical-modelling',[
 ['Nondimensionalization','Scaling removes units and exposes meaningful parameter ratios.','Use nonzero characteristic scales consistent with the domain.','Scale dN/dt=rN(1−N/K).',['Let u=N/K and τ=rt, with r,K>0.','The chain rule gives du/dτ=u(1−u).','Physical r and K remain the time and population scales.'],'For N=30,K=120, find u.',.25,'Removing units removes physical interpretation.','The scales map normalized predictions back to measurements.'],
 ['Identifiability','Distinct parameters are identifiable only if observable predictions distinguish them.','Identifiability depends on what is measured.','Observe y=abx with unknown a,b.',['The data identify the product ab.','Pairs (2,3) and (1,6) make identical predictions.','An extra measurement is needed to recover both separately.'],'For a=2,b=3,x=4, find y.',24,'Perfect fit determines every parameter.','Different parameter combinations may generate identical observations.'],
 ['Validation and residuals','Residual structure diagnoses model discrepancies; held-out data assess prediction.','Avoid leakage and specify the sampling process and distribution shifts.','Predictions 2,4,6; observations 3,4,8.',['Residuals are 1,0,2.','Squared-error sum is 5; training MSE is 5/3.','Unseen-data error requires separate validation.'],'Squared-error sum for residuals 1,0,2?',5,'Small training error guarantees reliable forecasts.','Overfitting and distribution changes can defeat forecasts.'],
 ['Sensitivity and uncertainty','A derivative measures local sensitivity; uncertainty propagation also needs input uncertainty and dependence.','Linear propagation is only a local approximation.','For y=x² near x=3, estimate a small change.',['The derivative is 6.','First-order output change is 6Δx.','The exact change also contains (Δx)².'],'First-order change for Δx=0.01?',.06,'A sensitivity derivative is a confidence interval.','Coverage needs a probability model, not only a derivative.']]);
extendCourse('discrete-world',['Prove statements using induction and invariants.','Connect counting, recurrences and modular algorithms.'],'Prove the first n odd numbers sum to n².','Base n=1 holds. Adding 2n+1 to n² gives (n+1)². Base and induction step establish every positive integer case.','/discrete-world',[
 ['Induction and invariants','An invariant holds initially and survives every allowed transition.','Test all transitions symbolically; examples alone are insufficient.','A process starts even and adds or subtracts 2.',['The start has form 2k.','Updates give 2(k±1).','Every reachable value is even.'],'Start at 4 and add 2 three times.',10,'Many successful examples prove an invariant.','A proof covers all allowed transitions.'],
 ['Recurrences and generating functions','A generating function encodes a sequence as coefficients.','Formal identities do not automatically establish numerical convergence.','Solve a₀=1,aₙ=2aₙ₋₁.',['Iteration gives aₙ=2ⁿ.','A(x)=1+2xA(x), so A(x)=1/(1−2x).','Numerical convergence requires |x|<1/2.'],'Find a₅.',32,'Formal algebra proves convergence for every x.','Coefficient identities and numerical evaluation differ.'],
 ['Inclusion–exclusion','Subtract intersections when counting overlapping sets.','Specify the universe and whether choices are ordered.','Of 30 students, 18 study A,15 study B,8 both.',['Union size is 18+15−8=25.','Five study neither.','Exactly one has size 18+15−16=17.'],'How many study exactly one?',17,'Counts can always be added directly.','Direct addition requires disjoint sets.'],
 ['Modular inverses','An inverse of a modulo m exists exactly when gcd(a,m)=1.','Modular division needs an invertible divisor.','Find inverse of 7 modulo 26.',['Euclid gives 26=3×7+5,7=5+2,5=2×2+1.','Back substitution gives 1=3×26−11×7.','Thus inverse is −11≡15.'],'Inverse of 3 modulo 7?',5,'Every nonzero modular element is invertible.','2 modulo 6 has no inverse.']]);
extendCourse('set-theory',['Translate quantifiers and construct counterexamples.','Connect partitions, functions and cardinality.'],'Prove f:A→P(A) cannot be surjective.','Let D={a∈A:a∉f(a)}. If f(d)=D, then d∈D iff d∉D, a contradiction. D is outside the image.','/set-theory',[
 ['Quantifiers and negation','Negation switches universal and existential quantifiers and negates the predicate.','Preserve their order; swapping changes meaning.','Negate: every real x has a real y>x.',['Change every x to there exists x.','Change there exists y to every y.','The negation is: some real x bounds every real y above.'],'Counterexamples needed to refute a universal claim?',1,'A y depending on x means one y works for all x.','Dependent choices are weaker than a common choice.'],
 ['Equivalence and partitions','Reflexive, symmetric, transitive relations partition sets into disjoint classes.','All three properties are required.','Partition integers modulo 3.',['Every integer has remainder 0,1 or 2.','Equal remainders define equivalence.','Three disjoint classes cover the integers.'],'Number of classes modulo 5?',5,'Symmetry alone gives equivalence.','Reflexivity and transitivity must also hold.'],
 ['Images and preimages','Preimages preserve intersections; images may fail because of input collisions.','Specify domain and codomain.','Use f(x)=x²,A={−1},B={1}.',['A∩B is empty, so its image is empty.','Both images are {1}.','Image of intersection differs from intersection of images.'],'Number of real preimages of 9?',2,'Images always preserve intersections.','Noninjective maps can merge distinct inputs.']]);
extendCourse('graph-theory',['Choose algorithms according to graph assumptions.','Certify optimality by cut and path arguments.'],'Why must every spanning tree contain a bridge?','Removing the bridge separates a cut with no other crossing edge. Any connected spanning subgraph must retain it.','/graph-theory',[
 ['Traversal and weights','BFS minimizes edge count; Dijkstra minimizes nonnegative weighted path cost.','Negative weights invalidate the standard settled-distance argument.','Compare weight-10 edge with weights 2 and 3 along two edges.',['The direct route has fewer edges.','The alternative costs 5.','Weighted optimization selects the alternative.'],'Cost of weights 2,3,4?',9,'Dijkstra works unchanged with negative edges.','Its correctness argument relies on nonnegative weights.'],
 ['Spanning trees and cuts','A lightest edge crossing a cut belongs to some MST.','Ties need not force an edge into every MST; connectivity is required.','Triangle weights 1,2,5.',['A spanning tree uses two edges.','Weights 1 and 2 connect without a cycle.','Minimum total is 3.'],'MST total for triangle weights 2,3,8?',5,'An MST is a shortest-path tree.','It minimizes total selected weight, not every source distance.'],
 ['Flow and cuts','Feasible flow respects capacities and conservation; a cut bounds its value.','Capacities are nonnegative; source and sink differ.','Independent paths have bottlenecks 3 and 2.',['Send 3 and 2 respectively.','Their bottleneck cut has capacity 5.','Flow matches the cut, proving optimality.'],'Flow on independent bottlenecks 4 and 6?',10,'Source outgoing capacity proves maximum flow.','Downstream cuts may be smaller.'],
 ['Planarity and Euler','Connected planar embeddings satisfy V−E+F=2; simple planar graphs with V≥3 satisfy E≤3V−6.','The edge bound is necessary, not sufficient.','Test K₅ with V=5,E=10.',['The planar bound is 9.','Ten edges violate it.','K₅ is nonplanar.'],'Faces for connected planar V=4,E=6?',4,'Passing the edge bound proves planarity.','Some nonplanar graphs pass it.']]);
extendCourse('probability-statistics',['Connect probability to sampling and design.','Interpret uncertainty without confusing association and causation.'],'A test reports p=0.03. What does this establish?','Under the null model and assumptions, outcomes at least as extreme have probability 0.03. This is not the probability the null is true, an effect-size estimate or a causal conclusion.','/probability-statistics',[
 ['Bayes and base rates','Posterior probability combines prevalence, sensitivity and false-positive probability.','Specify the population and test characteristics.','Prevalence 1%, sensitivity 90%, false-positive rate 5%.',['True-positive probability is 0.009.','False-positive probability is 0.0495.','Positive predictive value is 0.009/0.0585≈0.153846.'],'Expected true positives per 1000 people?',9,'90% sensitivity makes positive results 90% reliable.','Posterior reliability also depends on prevalence and false positives.'],
 ['Variance and dependence','Var(X+Y)=Var(X)+Var(Y)+2Cov(X,Y).','Moments must exist; uncorrelated need not mean independent.','X is equally likely −1 or 1 and Y=X.',['Each variance and their covariance equals 1.','X+Y=2X has variance 4.','Ignoring dependence incorrectly gives 2.'],'Var(3X) if Var(X)=2?',18,'Variances always add.','Dependence adds a covariance term.'],
 ['Sampling and CLT','The classical iid CLT concerns the standardized mean with finite variance.','Large samples do not remove selection bias; check independence and variance.','Population standard deviation 12, sample size 36.',['Variance of the mean is 144/36=4.','Standard error is 2.','Normal approximation needs additional distribution considerations.'],'Standard error for σ=15,n=25?',3,'The CLT makes original observations normal.','It concerns a standardized sample mean.'],
 ['Regression and multiple testing','Regression describes association; multiple testing increases false-discovery opportunities.','Check design, residuals, dependence and prespecified comparisons.','Twenty independent true-null tests at α=0.05.',['Expected false rejections equal 1.','Chance of at least one is 1−0.95²⁰≈0.642.','Bonferroni uses 0.0025 per test for family α=0.05.'],'Bonferroni threshold for 10 tests and family α=0.05?',.005,'A significant regression slope proves causation.','Confounding or selection may explain an association.']]);
extendCourse('differential-equations',['Check existence, uniqueness and stability.','Distinguish initial, boundary and numerical problems.'],'Solve y′=−2y,y(0)=3 and discuss Euler stability.','Separation gives 3e^(−2t), approaching zero. Forward Euler requires |1−2h|<1, hence 0<h<1, for asymptotic decay.','/differential-equations',[
 ['Existence and uniqueness','Continuity supports local existence; local Lipschitz behavior in y supports uniqueness.','These are local theorems on stated domains.','Analyze y′=√|y|,y(0)=0.',['The field is continuous but not locally Lipschitz at zero.','Zero and solutions growing as (t−T)²/4 after any T≥0 solve the forward IVP.','Uniqueness fails despite continuity.'],'Value of t²/4 at t=2?',1,'Continuity guarantees uniqueness.','The waiting-time example disproves that assertion.'],
 ['Numerical stability','Euler on y′=λy has update factor 1+hλ; accuracy and stability differ.','For negative real λ, asymptotic stability requires |1+hλ|<1.','For λ=−4 compare h=0.25 and 0.6.',['At 0.25 the factor is 0.','At 0.6 it is −1.4, causing growth.','The exact solution decays; growth is numerical.'],'Factor for λ=−2,h=0.1?',.8,'Consistency allows every step size.','Stiff decay can impose a severe stability restriction.'],
 ['Boundary eigenfunctions','Boundary conditions can restrict parameters and change uniqueness.','Specify interval and boundary type.','Solve y″+λy=0 on [0,π] with zero endpoints and nonzero y.',['For λ=k²>0, y(0)=0 removes cosine.','The other endpoint requires sin(kπ)=0.','Eigenvalues n² give eigenfunctions sin(nx).'],'Second positive eigenvalue?',4,'Every boundary problem has a unique solution.','Homogeneous eigenvalue problems admit nonzero solutions.'],
 ['Laplace and initial values','The transform of y′ is sY−y(0).','Check growth conditions and transform convergence.','Transform y′+2y=0,y(0)=3.',['sY−3+2Y=0.','Y=3/(s+2).','Inverse transform gives 3e^(−2t).'],'Numerator for initial value 5?',5,'Initial values disappear in transforms.','The derivative boundary term carries them.']]);
extendCourse('continued-fractions',['Construct exact convergents.','Connect periodicity, error bounds and Pell equations.'],'Find a Pell solution from √2.','√2=[1;2,2,…]. Its convergent 3/2 gives 3²−2×2²=1. Not every convergent solves the +1 equation.','/math-lab/continued-fractions',[
 ['Euclidean expansion','Euclidean quotients give a finite simple continued fraction.','Use a positive denominator and final-term convention.','Expand 43/19.',['43=2×19+5;19=3×5+4.','5=1×4+1;4=4×1.','The expansion is [2;3,1,4].'],'First partial quotient?',2,'Finite expansions are unique without a convention.','A final a>1 can be replaced by a−1 followed by 1.'],
 ['Convergent recurrence','pₙ=aₙpₙ₋₁+pₙ₋₂, with the same denominator recurrence; adjacent determinants are ±1.','Use p₋₂=0,p₋₁=1,q₋₂=1,q₋₁=0.','Compute [1;2,2].',['First terms give 1/1 and 3/2.','Next numerator is 2×3+1=7.','Next denominator is 2×2+1=5.'],'Denominator of [1;2,2,2]?',12,'Every plausible decimal approximation is a convergent.','Exact recurrences and determinant constraints characterize convergents.'],
 ['Periodicity and bounds','Eventually periodic simple expansions characterize quadratic irrationals.','After a₀ the infinite simple expansion has positive integer terms.','Approximate √2 by 7/5.',['Its square is 1.96.','The convergent bound is |√2−7/5|<1/25.','Actual error is about 0.014214; observation differs from proof.'],'Numerator of Pell solution 3²−2×2²=1?',3,'All irrationals have periodic continued fractions.','Only quadratic irrationals have eventual periodicity.']]);
extendCourse('famous-problems',['Distinguish theorem, conjecture and computational evidence.','Understand precise statements and proof scope.'],'What resolves an unbounded universal conjecture?','A proof covering all admissible cases, or one verified admissible counterexample. A finite search without a counterexample cannot establish the universal claim.','/math-lab/famous-problems',[
 ['Euclid and primes','Any proposed complete finite prime list yields a contradiction.','The constructed product plus one need not itself be prime.','Let N=p₁⋯pₖ+1 for a finite list.',['Every listed prime leaves remainder 1.','N>1 has a prime divisor.','That divisor is missing from the list.'],'N for list 2,3,5?',31,'A prime product plus one is always prime.','Only a new prime divisor is required; 30031=59×509 is composite.'],
 ['Irrationality proof','Lowest terms makes a parity contradiction decisive.','Use relatively prime numerator and positive denominator.','Prove √2 irrational.',['Assume √2=a/b; then a²=2b².','Thus a=2k, giving b²=2k².','Both a and b are even, contradicting lowest terms.'],'Coefficient of k² in (2k)²?',4,'Nonterminating decimals prove irrationality.','Rationals may have nonterminating periodic decimals.'],
 ['Open problems and evidence','Riemann and P versus NP have precise unresolved statements; computation covers finite cases.','Consult authoritative status sources; related theorems may not settle the full question.','Interpret a million checked cases.',['Validated computation establishes those cases.','Untested cases remain.','A theorem or reduction must bridge the logical gap.'],'Counterexamples needed to refute a universal conjecture?',1,'A large successful search proves the conjecture.','Finite verification has finite logical scope.']]);
extendCourse('stats-inference',['Choose procedures by estimand and design.','Interpret coverage, power and equivalence.'],'Plan a treatment analysis before observing outcomes.','Specify population, estimand, sampling or randomization, primary contrast, meaningful effect threshold, interval and multiplicity plan. Choose sample size using variability and power; state missing-data assumptions.','/math-lab/stats-inference',[
 ['Estimands and likelihood','Likelihood compares parameter values for observed data; an estimand is the target quantity.','Binomial likelihood needs fixed n, common p and independent trials.','Observe 6 successes in 10 trials.',['Likelihood is proportional to p⁶(1−p)⁴.','Log derivative is 6/p−4/(1−p).','The maximum occurs at p=0.6.'],'MLE for 3 successes in 12 trials?',.25,'Likelihood is automatically a posterior density.','A posterior also needs a prior and parameter-space normalization.'],
 ['Interval coverage','Confidence describes repeated-sampling procedure coverage. Wilson intervals avoid the degenerate boundary behavior of Wald intervals.','Nominal coverage may be approximate and depends on assumptions.','Inspect a Wald interval with 0 successes in 10.',['Estimate and plug-in standard error are zero.','Wald yields [0,0].','Score or exact methods give a nonzero upper bound.'],'Expected coverage count for 100 exactly nominal 95% intervals?',95,'A realized interval assigns 95% probability to a fixed parameter.','The 95% refers to the procedure over repeated samples.'],
 ['Power and equivalence','Power is rejection probability at a specified alternative; nonsignificance is not equivalence.','Equivalence requires prespecified meaningful margins.','Estimate 0.1 with CI [−0.8,1.0], margin ±0.5.',['The interval includes zero.','It also includes effects beyond the margin.','Equivalence has not been demonstrated.'],'Power when β=0.2?',.8,'Nonsignificance proves equality.','Low precision can hide meaningful differences.']]);
extendCourse('special-functions',['Use definitions and recurrences on valid domains.','Distinguish continuation from convergent series.'],'Does ζ(−1)=−1/12 make 1+2+3+… converge?','No. The defining Dirichlet series converges for Re(s)>1. The value at −1 is from analytic continuation; ordinary positive-integer partial sums diverge.','/math-lab/special-functions',[
 ['Gamma recurrence','Γ(z+1)=zΓ(z) extends factorials.','The defining integral needs Re(z)>0; continuation has poles at nonpositive integers.','Evaluate Γ(5).',['Γ(1)=1.','Recurrence gives 4×3×2×1.','Γ(5)=24=4!, not 5!.'],'Γ(4)?',6,'Γ(n)=n!.','For positive integers Γ(n)=(n−1)!.'],
 ['Legendre orthogonality','Inner products isolate coefficients; Legendre polynomials are orthogonal on [−1,1] with unit weight.','Interval and weight are essential.','Check P₀=1,P₁=x.',['Their product is x.','Its symmetric-interval integral is zero.','The squared norm of P₁ is 2/3.'],'Integral of P₀² on [−1,1]?',2,'Orthogonal means normalized.','Zero cross-products do not impose unit norm.'],
 ['Error function and normal CDF','erf(x)=(2/√π)∫₀ˣe^(−t²)dt; Φ(z)=(1+erf(z/√2))/2.','Standardize using (x−μ)/σ with σ>0.','Evaluate Φ(0).',['erf(0)=0.','Substitute into the relationship.','Φ(0)=1/2.'],'erf(0)?',0,'Density height is point probability.','Continuous point probabilities are zero; intervals require integration.'],
 ['Bessel local series','J₀(x)=1−x²/4+x⁴/64−… solves the order-zero Bessel equation.','Truncation needs an error analysis; independent solutions behave differently at zero.','Approximate J₀(1) through fourth degree.',['Constant term is 1.','Subtract 1/4 and add 1/64.','The approximation is 0.765625, not an exact value.'],'J₀(0)?',1,'A truncation is exact away from its center.','Omitted terms remain and determine the error.']]);
const addLesson=(id:string,seed:LessonSeed,href:string)=>{
 const [title,idea,conditions,problem,solution,question,result,mistake,explanation]=seed;
 masteryCourses[id].units.push(unit(title,idea,conditions,['State the assumptions and target quantity.','Follow the derivation in the worked example.','Check the conclusion and explain a boundary case.'],problem,solution,[question,result],mistake,explanation,'Predict a result before changing the lab. Compare observation with the derivation and record which assumptions remain valid.',href));
};
addLesson('calculus',['Taylor remainder and approximation','A Taylor polynomial needs a remainder bound to support an accuracy claim.','For the Lagrange remainder, bound the next derivative throughout the interval.','Approximate e^0.1 through degree 2.',['The polynomial is 1+0.1+0.005=1.105.','The third derivative is e^x≤e^0.1 on [0,0.1].','Error is ≤e^0.1×0.001/6<0.000185.'],'Quadratic Taylor approximation of e^0 at 0?',1,'A polynomial approximation equals the function exactly.','The remainder accounts for the difference.'],'/calculus');
addLesson('number-systems',['Countability and diagonal reasoning','A list of infinite digit sequences can be defeated by changing the nth digit of its nth entry.','Use a digit convention avoiding nonunique terminal expansions.','Can all infinite sequences of zeros and ones be listed?',['Assume sequence sₙ occupies row n.','Define digit dₙ=1−sₙ(n).','The new sequence differs from every row at its diagonal digit.'],'Number of length-4 binary sequences?',16,'Infinite sets all have the same cardinality.','A diagonal construction distinguishes countable from uncountable families.'],'/number-systems');
addLesson('number-systems',['Exact rationals and equivalent forms','A rational is represented by many pairs (a,b) with b nonzero; reducing gives a canonical positive-denominator form.','Never divide by a zero denominator.','Reduce −18/24.',['The gcd of 18 and 24 is 6.','Divide numerator and denominator by 6.','The reduced value is −3/4.'],'Positive reduced denominator of 18/24?',4,'Changing numerator alone preserves a fraction.','Equivalent forms scale numerator and denominator by the same nonzero factor.'],'/number-systems');
addLesson('linear-algebra',['Projection and QR geometry','Orthogonal projection decomposes a vector into a component in a subspace and an orthogonal residual.','Project onto a nonzero vector; Gram–Schmidt requires independent inputs or rank handling.','Project (3,4) onto (1,0).',['The scalar coefficient is ((3,4)·(1,0))/1=3.','Projection is (3,0).','Residual (0,4) is orthogonal to the direction.'],'Squared residual norm?',16,'Projection preserves every vector length.','Only vectors already in the subspace retain their full norm.'],'/linear-algebra');
addLesson('complex-numbers',['Roots and polar geometry','The nth roots of a nonzero complex number have equal modulus and equally spaced arguments.','Include all n branches; zero has a single distinct nth root.','Find cube roots of 8.',['The modulus of each root is 2.','Arguments are 0,2π/3,4π/3.','Roots are 2,−1+i√3,−1−i√3.'],'Number of distinct fourth roots of a nonzero number?',4,'Only the principal root solves the equation.','All argument branches give valid roots.'],'/complex-numbers');
addLesson('complex-numbers',['Cauchy integral formula','An analytic function inside and on a simple contour satisfies ∮f(z)/(z−a)dz=2πif(a).','The point a is inside, contour positive, and f analytic in a neighborhood of the closed region.','Integrate z²/(z−1) around |z|=2.',['The numerator f(z)=z² is entire.','The pole point 1 lies inside the circle.','The integral is 2πif(1)=2πi.'],'For numerator z³ and a=2 inside a radius-3 circle, coefficient of 2πi?',8,'The formula works when a lies on the contour.','A contour singularity makes the ordinary integral undefined without an additional prescription.'],'/complex-numbers');
addLesson('mathematical-modelling',['Constrained optimization','A numerical optimum must satisfy physical feasibility and the stated objective.','Inspect boundaries as well as stationary points.','Maximize profit P(x)=10x−x² for 0≤x≤3.',['The unconstrained stationary point is x=5.','It is infeasible; P increases throughout [0,3].','The constrained maximum is P(3)=21.'],'Maximum profit on [0,3]?',21,'An unconstrained optimum solves every constrained problem.','Constraints can move the optimum to a boundary.'],'/mathematical-modelling');
addLesson('discrete-world',['Complexity and exponential growth','Resource bounds describe growth with input size, not a single runtime measurement.','Specify the computational model and what n measures.','Compare enumerating all subsets with scanning n items.',['There are 2ⁿ subsets.','At n=10 there are 1024, versus ten scan steps.','Doubling n squares the subset count, while doubling the scan length.'],'Number of subsets of 8 items?',256,'Polynomial time always beats exponential time on every small input.','Constants and implementation affect finite sizes; asymptotic growth concerns large inputs.'],'/discrete-world');
addLesson('set-theory',['Function counting and edge cases','A function chooses one codomain value for each domain element, giving mⁿ functions between finite sets of sizes n and m.','The empty-domain case has exactly one function, including to an empty codomain.','Count functions from a 3-element set to a 2-element set.',['Each input has two independent choices.','Multiply 2×2×2.','There are eight functions, not all injective.'],'Functions from an empty set to a 4-element set?',1,'There are no functions with empty domain.','The empty graph is the unique function from the empty domain.'],'/set-theory');
addLesson('set-theory',['Order versus equivalence','A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive; incomparable pairs may exist.','Antisymmetry differs from symmetry.','Consider inclusion among subsets of {1,2}.',['Every set includes itself and inclusion is transitive.','Mutual inclusion forces equality.','{1} and {2} are incomparable, so the order is not total.'],'Number of subsets of {1,2}?',4,'Every partial order compares every pair.','That extra property defines a total order.'],'/set-theory');
addLesson('graph-theory',['Matching and Hall obstruction','A bipartite matching covers the left side only if every left subset has at least as many neighbors. Hall theorem also makes this condition sufficient.','Specify the bipartition; count distinct neighbors, not edges.','Three left vertices all connect only to two right vertices.',['Choose the full left set of size 3.','Its neighborhood has size 2.','Hall condition fails, so no matching covers all three.'],'Maximum matching size in this complete 3-by-2 graph?',2,'Many incident edges guarantee a perfect matching.','A shared small neighborhood can obstruct coverage.'],'/graph-theory');
addLesson('probability-statistics',['Study design and causality','Random assignment controls treatment confounding in expectation; random sampling addresses population representation.','Noncompliance, attrition and interference can complicate causal interpretation.','Compare volunteers choosing treatment with a randomized trial.',['Volunteer choice may correlate with baseline risk.','Random assignment breaks that link in expectation.','Randomization does not itself make volunteers representative of everyone.'],'Equal allocation of 80 participants gives how many per arm?',40,'Random assignment guarantees population representativeness.','Assignment and sampling address different questions.'],'/probability-statistics');
addLesson('differential-equations',['Heat equation and boundary modes','Separated modes solve the heat equation and decay according to their spatial eigenvalues.','Use specified boundary conditions and diffusivity κ>0; general initial data require a suitable series.','On [0,π], solve u_t=κu_xx with zero ends and initial sin(2x).',['The spatial second derivative is −4sin(2x).','The time coefficient obeys a′=−4κa.','The solution is e^(−4κt)sin(2x).'],'Decay-rate coefficient for mode sin(3x) when κ=1?',9,'Every PDE can be solved by one separated mode.','General data require combinations; geometry and operator matter.'],'/differential-equations/pde');
addLesson('continued-fractions',['Adjacent convergent determinants','Adjacent convergents differ by exactly 1 divided by the product of their denominators in absolute value.','Use consecutive convergents from the same simple expansion.','Compare 3/2 and 7/5.',['Cross determinant is 3×5−7×2=1.','Their absolute difference is 1/10.','The determinant also proves each fraction is reduced.'],'Absolute difference between 3/2 and 7/5?',.1,'Consecutive convergents can share a nontrivial numerator-denominator gcd.','Their ±1 determinant prevents such a common divisor.'],'/math-lab/continued-fractions');
addLesson('continued-fractions',['Pell signs and periodicity','Convergents of √d can solve x²−dy²=±1; the signs must be checked.','Use positive nonsquare integer d and exact integer arithmetic.','Test convergents 1/1,3/2,7/5 for √2.',['1²−2×1²=−1.','3²−2×2²=1.','7²−2×5²=−1; signs alternate here.'],'Value of 3²−2×2²?',1,'Every convergent solves the +1 Pell equation.','Some give −1, and the exact residual must be verified.'],'/math-lab/continued-fractions');
addLesson('famous-problems',['Euler tours and degree parity','An Euler circuit traverses each edge once; connected nonisolated support and even degrees characterize existence in an undirected graph.','Vertices may repeat; edges may not.','Does a triangle have an Euler circuit?',['Each vertex has degree 2.','The graph is connected.','Walking around the triangle uses each edge once and returns to the start.'],'Number of odd-degree vertices in a triangle?',0,'An Euler tour must visit each vertex only once.','That restriction concerns Hamiltonian cycles, a different problem.'],'/graph-theory');
addLesson('famous-problems',['Constructibility and algebraic degree','Straightedge-and-compass constructions generate towers of quadratic extensions.','A power-of-two degree is necessary, not sufficient for all algebraic numbers.','Can a unit cube be doubled by constructing its new side length?',['The required length is cube root of 2.','x³−2 is irreducible over ℚ, so its degree is 3.','Degree 3 is not a power of two, obstructing classical construction.'],'Algebraic degree of cube root of 2 over ℚ?',3,'Accurate drawing proves exact constructibility.','Approximation and an allowed exact construction are distinct.'],'/geometry');
addLesson('stats-inference',['Paired and independent comparisons','Paired data are analyzed through within-pair differences; independent samples need their own variance calculation.','Pairing must be meaningful and differences satisfy the chosen procedure assumptions.','Before values 4,8,10 and after 5,10,13.',['Differences are 1,2,3.','Mean difference is 2.','Its sample standard deviation is 1; standard error is 1/√3.'],'Mean paired difference?',2,'Pairing can be ignored without affecting uncertainty.','Within-pair dependence changes the variance of a difference.'],'/math-lab/stats-inference');
addLesson('stats-inference',['Multiplicity and practical effects','Family-wise error and false discovery rate answer different questions. Statistical significance does not measure practical importance.','Choose correction and meaningful effect thresholds before selecting results.','Ten tests share a family error target 0.05.',['Bonferroni assigns 0.005 per test.','The union bound controls family error regardless of dependence.','Report estimates and intervals alongside decisions.'],'Per-test Bonferroni threshold?',.005,'A small p-value guarantees an important effect.','Large samples can detect effects too small to matter.'],'/math-lab/stats-inference');
addLesson('special-functions',['Zeta domains and continuation','The zeta Dirichlet series converges absolutely for Re(s)>1; continuation extends the function but not that series.','There is a pole at s=1; separate function values from divergent sums.','Compare ζ(2) and ζ(−1).',['Σ1/n² converges and equals π²/6.','Σn diverges in the ordinary sense.','The continued value −1/12 is not that ordinary sum.'],'Real boundary value s at the zeta pole?',1,'Analytic continuation makes every original series converge.','It extends the function, not the original summation procedure.'],'/math-lab/special-functions');
extendCourse('advanced-differential-equations',['Analyze phase-plane equilibria and stiffness.','Connect transform, boundary and PDE methods to explicit hypotheses.'],'Explain why a stiff system challenges explicit Euler.','A rapidly decaying mode forces tiny stable time steps even when the slow observable changes gently. Implicit methods can relax stability restrictions but still need accuracy control.','/math-lab/differential-equations',[
 ['Linear systems and eigenmodes','Eigenvalues determine growth, decay and oscillation of linear modes.','Defective matrices require generalized eigenvectors; nonlinear linearization can be inconclusive at zero real parts.','Analyze x′=−x,y′=2y.',['Solutions are x=C₁e^(−t),y=C₂e^(2t).','One mode decays, the other grows.','The origin is a saddle, unstable overall.'],'Number of growing modes?',1,'One negative eigenvalue guarantees stability.','All modes matter; a positive-real-part mode causes instability.'],
 ['Stiff time scales','Stable explicit steps may be governed by the fastest mode.','For Euler and negative real λ use |1+hλ|<1.','Compare modes λ=−1 and −1000.',['The slow mode allows h<2.','The fast mode requires h<0.002.','The system must respect the more restrictive mode.'],'Fast-mode upper step bound for λ=−100?',.02,'Slow visible motion permits any large step.','Hidden fast modes can destabilize the numerical solution.'],
 ['Resonant forcing','Forcing at an undamped natural frequency produces secular growth.','Damping changes resonance behavior; distinguish homogeneous and forced solutions.','Solve y″+y=sin t with a particular solution.',['Try y_p=−(t/2)cos t.','Differentiation gives y_p″+y_p=sin t.','The amplitude grows with t, unlike a bounded homogeneous sinusoid.'],'Magnitude of the t coefficient in this particular solution?',.5,'Sinusoidal input always gives bounded sinusoidal output.','Undamped resonance produces growing amplitude.'],
 ['Boundary eigenvalues','Boundary data impose spectral conditions on separated spatial solutions.','Use the prescribed operator, interval and boundary type.','On [0,π], zero endpoints for X″+λX=0.',['Nonzero solutions require λ=n².','The first mode is sin x.','The second is sin 2x with eigenvalue 4.'],'Third eigenvalue?',9,'Initial-value uniqueness automatically applies to every boundary problem.','Boundary constraints have distinct existence and uniqueness conditions.'],
 ['Heat modes and decay','Each sine mode of the heat equation decays at rate κn².','This applies to zero Dirichlet ends on [0,π] and κ>0.','Initial state sin(2x), diffusivity κ=3.',['Spatial eigenvalue is 4.','Time coefficient solves a′=−12a.','Solution is e^(−12t)sin(2x).'],'Decay rate?',12,'Every spatial mode decays at the same rate.','Higher-frequency modes have larger n² decay rates.']]);

